<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Hardened runtime entitlements for the PyInstaller-built GHManage.app.

    Apple requires the hardened runtime (codesign's "options runtime") before
    notarization will accept a submission, and the hardened runtime breaks
    CPython unless the exceptions below are granted. Each one is load-bearing;
    do not trim this list without launching a notarized build on a Mac that has
    never run GHManage before. The failure mode for a missing entitlement is an
    app that dies before it can draw a window, with nothing in the UI to say
    why — you have to go read Console.app to find out.

    allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        CPython and several C extensions allocate writable-executable pages.
        Without this the interpreter dies at startup.

    disable-library-validation
        Library validation refuses to load any nested library not signed by the
        same Team ID. build.sh re-signs every .so and .dylib in the bundle, so
        in principle this is redundant — but wxPython alone contributes dozens
        of libraries, Velopack injects its own UpdateMac binary after we sign,
        and a single file missed anywhere in that set turns into a launch
        failure with no visible error. The exemption costs nothing here: the
        app loads no plugins and executes no third-party code at runtime.

    allow-dyld-environment-variables
        The PyInstaller bootloader sets DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH so the bundled
        libraries in Contents/Frameworks can be found.

    allow-jit
        Some bundled dependencies map JIT pages. Harmless when unused, and
        cheaper to include than to diagnose its absence.

    Deliberately NOT included: com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection.
    It is a far broader exemption, Apple discourages it, and nothing here needs it.

    This app is not sandboxed, so file and network access need no entitlement.
    Sandboxing is in fact incompatible with Velopack, whose updater has to write
    outside the sandbox and request elevation — both forbidden under App Sandbox.
    GHManage also shells out to the `gh` CLI, which a sandbox would block.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
